STATEMENT OF CHANGES (Apache License 2.0, section 4.b)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The original work includes no NOTICE file, so this document does not reproduce
one. It exists for the two things section 4.b of the license requires of any
derivative work: retaining the copyright notice above and stating the changes
made.

WebCircuits is not a copy of the original: it is a reimplementation. It has
been rewritten from C#/WPF to TypeScript on Canvas 2D so that it runs in a
browser instead of on Windows. Summary of the changes:

1. Language and platform. C# and WPF (Windows) are replaced by TypeScript and
   Canvas 2D (browser). The simulation engine uses bit masks instead of the
   original's representation.

2. Starting point. The code was read and translated from the commit pinned
   above. No compiled code or binaries were copied.

3. What is deliberately kept from the original. The data model, the simulation
   semantics, the ".CircuitProject" file format, the grid geometry and the
   behaviour of pins and gates are faithful to the original, so that existing
   circuits behave the same way.

4. What has been completely rewritten. The entire interface: colours,
   typography, panels, menus, icons and animations are new and are not meant to
   imitate the original's.

5. Features the original does not have, which have been added: layers with
   independent visibility, configurable themes and colours, browser-native file
   management, circuit synthesis from a truth table, propagation-delay
   analysis, buses that do not saturate, a deterministic clock and a teaching
   mode.

6. Elements derived directly from the original work. The ANTLR grammar files
   (.g4) and the XSD schemas come from the original source. The grammars are
   used only as a specification for a hand-written parser: neither generated
   code nor the ANTLR runtime is part of WebCircuits.

7. Name. It has been changed to "WebCircuits". The Apache License 2.0 grants no
   trademark rights, so continuing to use the name "LogicCircuit" would have
   been inappropriate.


